WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.177
Welcome to R&D Tax Workbench! In this full walkthrough, we'll prepare a complete R&D Tax

00:00:04.177 --> 00:00:07.753
Incentive claim for a fictional company, Kestrel Bioplastics, all the way from sign-up

00:00:07.753 --> 00:00:11.330
to the final schedule.

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:17.698
So why R&D Tax Workbench? First, speed. Upload your ledgers, payroll and timesheets, and

00:00:17.698 --> 00:00:22.263
go from raw data to a finished claim schedule, without weeks of back-and-forth.

00:00:22.713 --> 00:00:25.563
Second, cost. You pay as you go with credits, not thousands of dollars taken as a percentage

00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:28.413
of your refund.

00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:33.668
Third, it's built to stand up to review. The Division 355 rules, from feedstock and grant

00:00:33.668 --> 00:00:38.473
clawbacks to associates and overseas work, are built into every calculation.

00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:42.053
Fourth, your data stays yours. Your claim data is stored on your own computer, and AI

00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:45.183
processing stays in Australia.

00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:48.973
And fifth, it's made for accountants as well as businesses. One tool for every client's

00:00:48.973 --> 00:00:52.313
claim, year after year. Let's dive in!

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.312
Getting started takes seconds. Create your account with a name, an email address and a

00:00:57.312 --> 00:00:59.857
password.

00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:04.267
Your claim data is stored in a database on your own computer, not on a third-party server.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:08.177
Only the text an AI feature needs is sent for processing.

00:01:28.647 --> 00:01:31.847
And we're in. The dashboard is ready for your first claim.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.760
The AI features, like narratives and account classification, run on credits. Open the

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.920
credit meter, pick a top-up amount, and pay securely through Stripe.

00:01:46.066 --> 00:01:48.846
Payment done, and the balance updates instantly.

00:01:50.866 --> 00:01:54.136
Let's create the claim for our fictional company, Kestrel Bioplastics. Enter the company

00:01:54.136 --> 00:01:57.406
name, the ABN and the income year.

00:01:58.766 --> 00:02:02.841
Next comes aggregated turnover. Kestrel's is just over $12 million, under the $20 million

00:02:02.841 --> 00:02:06.916
threshold for the refundable offset.

00:02:09.206 --> 00:02:13.969
The claim is created. On the company details page we add the address and the tax rate,

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:18.732
and complete the base rate entity test. Passive income is well under the 80% limit, confirming

00:02:18.732 --> 00:02:23.496
a 25% tax rate and a 43.5% offset.

00:02:29.532 --> 00:02:32.742
Every claim is built around projects. Instead of filling in long forms, paste your R&D

00:02:32.742 --> 00:02:35.952
notes, or upload your documents.

00:02:37.152 --> 00:02:42.032
The AI structures the project and proposes core and supporting activities. It also flags

00:02:42.032 --> 00:02:46.912
what to check: the overseas finding needed for the New Zealand trials, the feedstock adjustment

00:02:46.912 --> 00:02:51.792
for the pellets that were sold, the associate rule, and how the government grant is treated.

00:02:52.192 --> 00:02:54.912
You stay in control: review the suggestions, then create the project and its activities

00:02:54.912 --> 00:02:57.632
in one step.

00:02:58.032 --> 00:03:01.462
The narrative builder drafts every section, in the structure AusIndustry expects, using

00:03:01.462 --> 00:03:04.892
the facts and figures from your notes.

00:03:05.292 --> 00:03:09.857
Each activity gets a complete, section-by-section narrative, within the character limits.

00:03:09.857 --> 00:03:14.422
Values the AI had to infer are highlighted, so you know exactly what to verify.

00:03:14.922 --> 00:03:17.852
No more blank pages. Every narrative follows the structure AusIndustry expects, built

00:03:17.852 --> 00:03:20.782
from your own notes.

00:03:36.692 --> 00:03:40.432
Supporting activities are linked to the core activities they support.

00:03:40.932 --> 00:03:43.892
And the AI works within built-in Division 355 rules, like overseas findings and associate

00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:46.852
timing.

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:56.252
If an activity pauses this year, you can discontinue it, with its history kept. And every

00:03:56.252 --> 00:04:00.172
narrative exports to a single Word document for review.

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:03.577
Every narrative stays editable, with a full version history, so your technical team can

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:06.482
review and sign off.

00:04:23.931 --> 00:04:27.756
Now for payroll. Upload the payroll spreadsheet, and the AI maps the columns, checks the

00:04:27.756 --> 00:04:31.581
data, and confirms super against the guarantee rate.

00:04:32.081 --> 00:04:36.481
Payroll in any layout. The AI maps the columns, and remembers them for next time.

00:04:43.331 --> 00:04:48.591
Seven employees and $985,000 of payroll, imported in one step.

00:04:49.491 --> 00:04:53.867
Next, the timesheet. Hours are matched to each activity straight from the activity column,

00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:58.244
and part-timers are measured against their own hours. Sam works three days a week, so

00:04:58.244 --> 00:05:02.621
950 hours is 80.1% of his time.

00:05:04.484 --> 00:05:07.994
The combined view shows salary and super flowing to each activity. Anyone above 95% is

00:05:07.994 --> 00:05:11.504
named, so you can make sure the records support it, and anyone with no R&D time is listed

00:05:11.504 --> 00:05:15.014
too.

00:05:16.931 --> 00:05:20.981
Now the financials. First, a deliberately broken ledger. Bad rows are rejected, with the

00:05:20.981 --> 00:05:25.031
reason for each one, and nothing is imported until you confirm.

00:05:28.884 --> 00:05:32.344
Then the real general ledger. The hybrid parser reads every account and every transaction,

00:05:32.344 --> 00:05:35.804
and the AI classifies each account.

00:05:42.287 --> 00:05:46.542
Add the trial balance. Each source is stored separately, and reconciled against the others.

00:05:46.542 --> 00:05:50.797
This ledger is an expenses-only extract, so we mark it as partial.

00:05:51.197 --> 00:05:55.137
Then the profit and loss, saved with a single click, and the balance sheet.

00:05:55.637 --> 00:05:59.637
Load ledgers straight from your accounting export, as CSV, Excel or PDF.

00:06:15.664 --> 00:06:20.239
Every source now ties out, and the classification view shows what's in the claim. The

00:06:20.239 --> 00:06:24.814
New Zealand lab is shown, but kept out of the total until an overseas finding covers it.

00:06:29.904 --> 00:06:33.270
Patent filing costs are kept out, and anything excluded by law, such as interest, buildings

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.637
and core technology, is flagged with guidance on the rules. Every line stays under your

00:06:36.637 --> 00:06:40.004
control.

00:06:41.964 --> 00:06:46.404
Apportionment is where claims are often challenged. Salaries follow the timesheets to

00:06:46.404 --> 00:06:50.844
the dollar, and shared costs use the full-time equivalent ratio. Choose a method for each

00:06:50.844 --> 00:06:55.284
cost type, like floor space for rent, and machine hours for equipment.

00:06:55.784 --> 00:06:58.149
Change a method or a floor area, and every figure that depends on it updates straight

00:06:58.149 --> 00:07:00.514
away.

00:07:06.964 --> 00:07:10.209
Your reasoning goes in the notes column, and becomes your audit defence. There's also

00:07:10.209 --> 00:07:13.454
a built-in decline in value calculator.

00:07:17.964 --> 00:07:20.999
Contractors next. Add each one with its claimable percentage, and flag registered research

00:07:20.999 --> 00:07:24.034
service providers.

00:07:24.534 --> 00:07:29.174
Each contractor's claimable amount is worked out as you type, before anything is saved.

00:07:38.044 --> 00:07:43.304
Raman Advisory is owned by the chief scientist, so it's an associate. Only the amount

00:07:43.304 --> 00:07:48.564
actually paid during the year counts, so we record the $60,000 paid of the $90,000 invoiced.

00:07:49.064 --> 00:07:53.654
Timing rules like this are easy to get wrong in a spreadsheet. Here, they're built in.

00:08:00.084 --> 00:08:03.049
Overseas work stays at zero until an overseas finding covers it, with a clear warning

00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.014
so nothing slips through.

00:08:14.164 --> 00:08:18.639
The associate compliance check then maps every contractor to the right line of the schedule,

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:23.114
and carries the unpaid $30,000 forward to the year it's paid.

00:08:30.364 --> 00:08:33.509
Other expenses follow the apportionment you set, with materials, overheads, feedstock

00:08:33.509 --> 00:08:36.654
and depreciation in one place.

00:08:37.154 --> 00:08:40.424
And every amount traces back to the ledger account it came from.

00:08:47.164 --> 00:08:50.559
Kestrel sold pellets from its pilot batches, so the feedstock analysis checks the current

00:08:50.559 --> 00:08:53.954
rules and works out the adjustment.

00:08:54.354 --> 00:09:01.534
$75,000 of feedstock inputs against $180,000 of sales gives a Part B feedstock adjustment

00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:08.714
of $55,500. The analysis also flags the energy used on the pilot line for you to review.

00:09:10.514 --> 00:09:16.774
Kestrel also received a $150,000 state grant. Record it, add this year's claim with one

00:09:16.774 --> 00:09:23.034
click, and the recoupment clawback is calculated: $111,000 of extra assessable income.

00:09:23.534 --> 00:09:26.064
Feedstock and grant clawbacks are easy to miss. Here, they're calculated and reported

00:09:26.064 --> 00:09:28.594
in Part B.

00:09:45.697 --> 00:09:50.273
Finally, the summary. Every Part A line shows exactly where it came from, and every source

00:09:50.273 --> 00:09:54.850
now reconciles. Kestrel's notional R&D deductions come to $1.3 million, for a refundable

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:59.427
offset of $566,000.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:06.626
The Division 355 review checks the at-risk rule, overseas activities, exempt entity control

00:10:06.626 --> 00:10:10.923
and balancing adjustments. It asks you to confirm how the grant is treated, and confirms

00:10:10.923 --> 00:10:15.220
the New Zealand work needs an overseas finding.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.015
Each finding comes with the section of the law behind it, so you know exactly what to

00:10:18.015 --> 00:10:20.310
check.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:32.185
The schedule is laid out like the ATO form. Part B brings the feedstock and grant clawbacks

00:10:32.185 --> 00:10:36.260
together, and the net cash benefit allows for the tax on them.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:41.390
And the same figures flow through to the export page, the Excel workbook and the PDF for

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.190
your tax agent.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.800
That's the full workflow, from raw ledgers and timesheets to an R&D tax schedule. Thanks

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.870
so much for watching, and find out more at rdinnovate.com.
